{"type":"standard","title":"Riemann–Hurwitz formula","displaytitle":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Riemann–Hurwitz formula</span></span>","namespace":{"id":0,"text":""},"wikibase_item":"Q494995","titles":{"canonical":"Riemann–Hurwitz_formula","normalized":"Riemann–Hurwitz formula","display":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Riemann–Hurwitz formula</span></span>"},"pageid":497413,"lang":"en","dir":"ltr","revision":"1356576403","tid":"6a589a56-5ab4-11f1-bdae-7faefa1c02b7","timestamp":"2026-05-28T16:43:55Z","description":"Mathematical formula of two surfaces","description_source":"local","content_urls":{"desktop":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann%E2%80%93Hurwitz_formula","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann%E2%80%93Hurwitz_formula?action=history","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann%E2%80%93Hurwitz_formula?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Riemann%E2%80%93Hurwitz_formula"},"mobile":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann%E2%80%93Hurwitz_formula","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:History/Riemann%E2%80%93Hurwitz_formula","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann%E2%80%93Hurwitz_formula?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Riemann%E2%80%93Hurwitz_formula"}},"extract":"In mathematics, the Riemann–Hurwitz formula, named after Bernhard Riemann and Adolf Hurwitz, describes the relationship of the Euler characteristics of two surfaces when one is a ramified covering of the other. It therefore connects ramification with algebraic topology, in this case. It is a prototype result for many others, and is often applied in the theory of Riemann surfaces and algebraic curves.","extract_html":"<p>In mathematics, the <b>Riemann–Hurwitz formula</b>, named after Bernhard Riemann and Adolf Hurwitz, describes the relationship of the Euler characteristics of two surfaces when one is a <i>ramified covering</i> of the other. It therefore connects ramification with algebraic topology, in this case. It is a prototype result for many others, and is often applied in the theory of Riemann surfaces and algebraic curves.</p>"}